WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.260
Alright, let's jump right into this explainer for your next core stage. We're going to unpack the absolute ultimate skill that makes or breaks a product manager. If you've ever felt like you're doing literally everything right on paper, but you still just can't get your team to move in the same direction, well, you're definitely in the right place. Today, we're talking about influence without authority. This breakdown completely shatters the myth that logic and perfect product specs win the day. It reveals a pretty shocking finding from the early 20th century about what actually

2
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:36.020
drives professional success. Even in highly technical engineering fields, a mere 15% of

3
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.980
financial success was attributed to technical knowledge. A massive 85% came down to skill in

4
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.340
handling people. I mean, we often think that if our data is flawless, people will just naturally

5
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.840
fall in line. But human beings don't run purely on logic, right? They run on emotion. If you try

6
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.020
to persuade your teen using only cold, hard facts, you've probably lost the battle before the

7
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.150
discussion even starts. So let's look at the product manager's dilemma. Brilliant specs,

8
00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.830
stalled teams. As a PM, you're sitting in what might just be the most political role in the

9
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:11.710
entire building. You carry massive responsibility for the success of the product, but you have

10
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:16.210
absolute zero command authority over the developers, the designers, or the stakeholders

11
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.860
you rely on every single day. Take our running case study for this stage of the course. Meet

12
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.260
Mahal, a product manager at a company called Velox. On paper, she's doing everything by the

13
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:33.620
book. Excellent analytical skills? Check. Incredibly tidy documentation? Check. Solid data? You bet.

14
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.680
But despite all that, her team is dragging their feet, and she's getting some pretty stinging peer

15
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:45.160
feedback, saying she's, quite simply, hard to work with. It hit her hard. But Mahal had a crucial

16
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:51.570
realization. Her massive gap wasn't analytical. It was human. To figure this out, Mahal turned to

17
00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:57.650
a nearly century-old guide by a public speaking instructor named Dale Carnegie. Now, you might be

18
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:03.110
wondering, why does such an old book open a modern product course? Well, because without even knowing

19
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.930
it, Carnegie wrote the original playbook for leading without authority. For this course,

20
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:13.070
we've completely revamped his insights into modern PM terminology. It's basically a foundational

21
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:18.210
toolkit built entirely on trust, listening, and understanding the other side's motivation,

22
00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:23.050
rather than relying on a corporate hierarchy that, frankly, you don't even have access to anyway.

23
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.610
Connection before persuasion.

24
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.030
The real trick here is understanding the foundational techniques Michael applied

25
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.290
to start fixing those broken team dynamics.

26
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.630
Because before you can persuade anyone to build your roadmap, you have to build a real connection.

27
00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:42.990
People decide if they want to cooperate with you way before they ever evaluate the quality of your arguments.

28
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.390
So Michael put three modernized influence principles into practice.

29
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.230
First up, you have to stop criticizing the past.

30
00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.170
Criticism just makes people defensive and completely blocks any chance of meaningful change.

31
00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.230
You've got to focus entirely on the future.

32
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.630
Second, offer specific fact-based appreciation.

33
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.170
And we're not talking about generic flattery here.

34
00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.290
People can spot fake interest a mile away.

35
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:11.690
If you can't point to a specific factual thing you appreciate about a team member's work, honestly, it's better to just stay silent.

36
00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.790
And third, tie your requests directly to the team's internal motivations.

37
00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.010
Figure out what your developers actually want to learn or achieve.

38
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:23.010
Maybe a new feature is their chance to play with a technology they've been eager to try out.

39
00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.830
Internal motivation beats external persuasion every single time.

40
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:32.170
You can see this play out perfectly during a rather embarrassing situation for McCall.

41
00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.830
Velox accidentally shipped a release with a glaring bug.

42
00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.550
Now, the natural urge there is to point fingers, right?

43
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.790
Asking things like, how did you miss this bug?

44
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.750
But during the retro, McCall swallowed that urge.

45
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.210
Instead, she used a process-focused approach.

46
00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.710
She asked, which step in our process would have caught this in time?

47
00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.630
By asking a future-oriented process question instead of playing the blame game,

48
00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.370
she completely eliminated defensiveness in the room.

49
00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.350
And the result?

50
00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.970
The team actually proposed implementing automated tests entirely on their own.

51
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.050
And she even publicly praised the developer who traced the root cause.

52
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.860
Next up, let's dive into connecting and persuading.

53
00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:12.280
Winning without arguing.

54
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:18.840
Being pleasant is all well and good, but what happens when an executive makes a demand that will completely wreck your timeline?

55
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.340
How do you push back without resorting to exhausting, bridge-burning logic battles?

56
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:30.000
Because remember, if you win an argument purely on logic but make the other person feel inferior, you've actually lost.

57
00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:38.500
Let's see how Michal handled a total crisis when her VP of sales demanded a massive roadmap derailing feature just to close a quarter.

58
00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.560
The traditional approach would be to build a counter deck filled with logic and data to prove him wrong,

59
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.280
which, let's face it, almost always results in a defensive argument.

60
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.660
But Mahal used the new playbook.

61
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:50.620
She didn't argue.

62
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.960
First, she validated his strategic goal, agreeing that closing the quarter and pleasing the customer absolutely mattered.

63
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.080
Then she asked a brilliant trade-off question.

64
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.160
If we push other features back for this one, what do we tell the 10 customers waiting on the original plan?

65
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:07.800
She didn't tell him he was wrong.

66
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.700
She just helped him see the whole board.

67
00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.240
The stakeholder reaction was amazing.

68
00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:18.780
The VP paused, recalculated, and ended up proposing a much smaller, scoped-down interim solution himself.

69
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.400
Let's move to changing behavior, turning resistors into partners.

70
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.720
Because what happens when you face the absolute hardest challenge in product management?

71
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.140
I'm talking about changing the behavior of a stubborn veteran team member who pushes back on absolutely everything you propose.

72
00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:40.410
This is exactly what Mahal faced with Amir, a stubborn veteran developer who constantly

73
00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.850
questioned her prioritization and complained any time the direction changed.

74
00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.510
So for their next meeting, Mahal did something totally radical.

75
00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:52.310
She completely ditched her prepared product requirements document and held a one-on-one

76
00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.530
focused entirely on three simple steps.

77
00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:59.950
Step one, she asked what was bothering him and listened without interrupting for a full

78
00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:00.770
20 minutes.

79
00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.610
Step two, she showed honest appreciation,

80
00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.810
telling him his experience with the product's architecture was worth its weight in gold.

81
00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:12.430
And step three, she shared her retention challenge with him as a partner asking for help,

82
00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.490
rather than a boss handing down a speck.

83
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:19.010
Because she made him feel like a respected partner and protected his dignity,

84
00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.230
Amir completely opened up.

85
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.590
In fact, he proposed three brand new technical solutions to the retention problem

86
00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.150
that Michal hadn't even considered.

87
00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.230
In the next team meeting, she gave Amir full public credit for the ideas.

88
00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.990
And within just two weeks, Amir transformed from her absolute biggest critic into her strongest champion.

89
00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:38.950
She realized a powerful lesson.

90
00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.810
As a PM, you don't need to win every interaction.

91
00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:43.550
You just need to share the ownership.

92
00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:49.990
So as you get ready to dive into the reading material for this stage and truly master these exact frameworks,

93
00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.650
I want you to ask yourself a critical question.

94
00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.670
When you face resistance in your next sprint, and trust me, you will,

95
00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:58.130
How are you going to react?

96
00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.310
Are you going to try to win the argument and prove you're right?

97
00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.730
Or will you stop, listen, and try to understand what your team truly wants?

98
00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.510
Influence is basically compound interest.

99
00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.170
Every conversation you handle well builds the capital you're going to need for the next crisis.

100
00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.090
Dive into the stage materials, start practicing these habits tomorrow morning,

101
00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.590
and watch how quickly your team dynamics transform.

102
00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:20.850
See you in the course.

